# --- Lesson plans for grades 6-12, written at request time (FEAT-080) ---
#
# OFF by default and inert until flipped: with LP_612_ENABLED unset, grades 6-10
# keep serving the Oxbridge catalogue exactly as they do today, grades 11-12 do
# not appear on the picker at all, and no authoring job can be enqueued.
#
# When on, tapping a subtopic looks for a cached PDF in R2 under
# (segment_id, lang, template_version); a miss authors the lesson with the model
# below, renders it, caches it and sends it. First hit is ~2-3 minutes typically
# and has been measured up to ~10 with a full revision ladder; every hit after
# that is instant. Requires the segment corpus to be loaded --
# `node bot/scripts/import-lp612-segments.js <dir>`.
#
# Only the exact string "true" enables either flag.
LP_612_ENABLED=

# SEPARATE, and deliberately so. Islamiat books and seerah content anywhere in
# the corpus are held pending a native-speaker review; turning the feature on
# above must not release them. Leave EMPTY until that review has happened.
LP_612_RELIGIOUS_ENABLED=

# The language step: after the segment tap, the teacher chooses the plan's
# language («اردو / English») per request — nothing is written to her stored
# preference. The flag is the "served" switch for the deploy-before-republish
# window: deploy code with it off (inert), publish Flow v3.1 (the extra screen
# is valid-but-unreturned), verify the WABA carries it, THEN flip. Rollback is
# this flag alone — no deploy, no republish. Only the exact string "true"
# enables it, and it only ever narrows LP_612_ENABLED.
LP_612_LANG_MENU=

# The teacher EDIT lane: she replies to a delivered 6-12 lesson asking for a
# change ("make the homework shorter", "add another activity") and gets her own
# adjusted copy back. A THIRD flag, separate for the same reason the religious
# hold is separate — turning 6-12 lessons on must not silently turn on a path
# that spends money and rewrites a document every time a teacher replies.
#
# With this unset the follow-up is still RECOGNISED and answered honestly ("I
# cannot change a lesson yet"), which is already better than the generic reply
# she gets from a model that has never seen her lesson. What stays off is the
# authoring. Only the exact string "true" enables it, and it only ever narrows
# LP_612_ENABLED.
LP_612_EDIT_ENABLED=

# Which model writes the lesson. Part of no cache key and read fresh on every
# job, so changing it is an env change with no deploy and no code change.
# Routed through llm-client.js (OpenRouter), like every other model call here.
LP_AUTHOR_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5

# PER-FAMILY OVERRIDE — the maths/physics pilot (bd-u6za9). When set, books whose
# subject family is `maths` (mathematics AND physics) author on THIS model while
# every other family stays on LP_AUTHOR_MODEL.
#
# Why only that family: the 2026-09-03 bake-off had deepseek-v4-flash author a
# Grade 9 physics lesson lint-clean on the FIRST pass, no revision ladder, in
# 59.9s for $0.0036 — where claude-sonnet-5 produced no clean cell in the round.
# But one clean cell is an existence proof, not a rate (dsflash is 1/5 clean
# overall, with volatile latency), so it runs where it already wins and prose
# stays on sonnet — a global maths preamble was measured making Urdu prose 73%
# WORSE.
#
# UNSET THIS TO REVERT. Every family falls back to LP_AUTHOR_MODEL, with no
# deploy and no code change.
LP_AUTHOR_MODEL_MATHS_PHYSICS=

# Which author harness runs: `standard` (the v3 brief, today's serving path) or
# `flash` (per-family brief + mechanical repairs, built and measured FOR the flash
# models). Leave BLANK: the tier is then derived from the model id, so a flash
# model gets the flash harness and sonnet keeps the standard one. Set it only to
# pin one side of an A/B — a typo raises rather than silently authoring on the
# other harness and being scored as this one.
LP612_AUTHOR_TIER=

# Part of the R2 cache key. Bumping it misses every cached render rather than
# serving a stale layout; rolling it back re-serves the old ones instantly,
# because nothing is ever deleted.
LP_612_TEMPLATE_VERSION=v9.1

# Revision rounds on the author ladder before we serve the best draft we have.
LP612_AUTHOR_ROUNDS=3
# Hard stop for one authoring run. Must stay BELOW the SQS visibility extension
# in sqs-worker.js (900s) so the job apologises to the teacher before SQS hands
# the same lesson to a second worker.
LP612_AUTHOR_TIMEOUT_MS=720000
# How long before a teacher still waiting gets a second message instead of
# silence.
LP612_FOLLOWUP_MS=180000

# WHEN A LESSON NOBODY EVER PICKED UP IS WRITTEN OFF.
#
# A BACKSTOP, NOT A DETECTOR, and deliberately not on the authoring clock (bd-dr216). A render row
# with no picked_up_at is sitting in the queue, and queue wait is not failure: the sweep used to
# measure a run's age from started_at -- which is stamped at ENQUEUE -- and condemned jobs at ~17
# minutes that no worker had yet touched, on a lane whose measured p90 enqueue-to-done is 1023s.
#
# The only row this can honestly catch is one whose enqueue never landed at all, and the serving
# path now writes ENQUEUE_FAILED on those at the moment it happens, so what is left here is the
# residue: a process dying between the claim and the enqueue. Six hours is ~21x the worst queue
# wait we have measured, which is where a backstop belongs. Lower it only if you have evidence of
# a queue wait longer than a few minutes being IMPOSSIBLE -- otherwise it starts condemning
# healthy lessons again, which is the bug it exists to undo.
LP612_QUEUE_ABANDON_MS=21600000

# HOW OFTEN A STRANDED LESSON IS NOTICED.
#
# Shared with the coaching stale-session sweep further down this file — one interval drives both
# (bot/workers/sqs-worker.js). It is repeated here because its teacher-facing consequence in this
# lane is different and much worse, and nothing beside the LP612 vars said so.
#
# A worker killed mid-authoring (a deploy, and this deployment deploys on every merge) leaves a
# render row at status='authoring' with nobody coming back for it. Until the reaper transitions
# that row to 'failed', requestLesson reads it as "someone else is already paying for this one",
# so EVERY later tap on that lesson is told it is being written and nothing ever arrives. The
# sweep is what ends that, and this interval bounds how long it lasts. The row must first age past
# the SQS envelope measured FROM PICKUP (2x LP612_AUTHOR_TIMEOUT_MS, the heartbeat ceiling, plus
# the 900s the last visibility extension bought, plus a 3-minute grace) and is then found on the
# NEXT sweep. That window is wide on purpose: below it the message is still legitimately in flight
# and the worker holding it is provably alive, and reaping there is what made rows flip failed and
# then back to ready when the job finished (bd-w36m5). This interval is the only part of the delay
# we control cheaply, so it is the part worth setting.
#
# SET IT EXPLICITLY IN PRODUCTION — do not inherit the 15-minute default. Half an hour of a
# lesson lying about its own state is not a teacher-humane number, and the sweep is one indexed
# query against a small table, so running it often costs effectively nothing. 2 is the
# recommended production value; staging runs 1. Floor is 1 minute.
#
# Note this also means the configuration we load-tested (staging, 1 minute) is NOT the
# configuration production would run on the default — prod's recovery would be materially
# slower than anything we have measured.
STALE_RECOVERY_INTERVAL_MINUTES=2

# The model that labels a teacher's reply after a 6-12 lesson lands: is she asking
# for a CHANGE to the document, asking a QUESTION about it, saying thanks, or
# asking for something this feature cannot do? Deliberately a cheap model — the
# schema already stops an out-of-scope request from producing a harmful document,
# so this buys HONESTY ("that is not a change I can make"), not safety. Its
# failure falls toward answering her question, never toward editing on a guess.
# Unset uses the code default.
LP612_EDIT_INTENT_MODEL=

# Where the author reads printed-page text from. Set to a local directory for
# development; unset in production, where pages are fetched from R2 under
# lp612/page-truth/<book_stem>/. Upload with
# `node bot/scripts/upload-lp612-page-truth.js <page-truth-root>`.
LP612_PAGE_TRUTH_DIR=

# Which browser renders the PDF. The renderer needs Chromium; on macOS it uses
# the installed Google Chrome ("chrome"), and everywhere else — Railway included
# — it defaults to Playwright's own bundled browser. Set to "bundled" to force
# the bundled one, or to a Playwright channel name to pick a system browser.
# Leave EMPTY unless a Railway render turns out to need it.
LP612_CHROME_CHANNEL=
# Absolute path to a Chrome/Chromium binary for the CLI fallback path, when the
# Playwright launch is unavailable. Leave empty in normal operation.
LP612_CHROME_BIN=

# bd-htueq: max Chromium renders running AT ONCE, PER PROCESS. render_lp.js launches a fresh
# browser per gate/final render with nothing bounding how many run together — under load that is
# up to ~15 concurrent Chromium instances fighting one container's CPU, RAM and the 64MB
# /dev/shm a single render already runs close to (see bot/vendor/lp-v9/SYNC.md §3.7), which is
# the measured cause of a load-test latency blowup. This is a PER-PROCESS cap: fleet-wide
# concurrent renders = this number x replica count, not x total worker jobs. Default is 2 — see
# lp612-render.service.js for the full justification. Leave EMPTY to use the default.
LP612_RENDER_CONCURRENCY=

# bd-htueq: a render whose total time (queue wait + the render itself) exceeds 2x this many ms
# emits lp612.render.slow telemetry. No per-render latency baseline exists yet — this default
# (10000ms) is a conservative starting point, not a measurement, chosen so a solo uncontended
# render (single-digit seconds) stays quiet while real tail contention is surfaced immediately.
# Tune once the event itself has produced real numbers. Leave EMPTY to use the default.
LP612_RENDER_EXPECTED_MS=

